// FB_Timers_Counters — body only.
// TIA's block editor already knows this is FUNCTION_BLOCK
// "FB_Timers_Counters" from the interface table; do NOT type
// FUNCTION_BLOCK, VAR/END_VAR, BEGIN, or END_FUNCTION_BLOCK in the code
// pane — paste/type only the statements below. Declare all Static
// variables (listed in docs/README_PLC_Tags.md) via the interface table's
// Static section first.
//
// Dual-driven: every trigger (Start/CU/CD/Reset/Load) can come from a
// physical input OR its HMI memory-tag counterpart (both live in the PLC
// tags table, TagTable_Timers_Counters.md, OR'd together below) — either
// path fires the same block. Outputs (Q/QU/QD) are written to physical
// %Q tags, which the HMI status circles bind to directly, so there's one
// source of truth for state.
//
// NOTE 1: plain TON/TOF/TP (resolved to TON_TIME/TOF_TIME/TP_TIME) have
// NO "R" (Reset) input in this TIA/S7-1500 library version — confirmed by
// compiler ("formal parameter 'R' is invalid"). Reset is folded into IN
// instead: IN := Start AND NOT Reset. Caveat: this resets TON instantly,
// delays TOF's Q drop by PT after Reset is pressed, and cannot interrupt
// an already-running TP pulse (TP ignores IN once triggered).
// NOTE 2: CTD/CTUD's Load input formal parameter is named "LD" in TIA/
// S7-1500 SCL, not "LOAD" — using "LOAD" fails to compile.

#Preset_TON := DINT_TO_TIME(REAL_TO_DINT(#HMI_PT_TON_s * 1000.0));
#Preset_TOF := DINT_TO_TIME(REAL_TO_DINT(#HMI_PT_TOF_s * 1000.0));
#Preset_TP := DINT_TO_TIME(REAL_TO_DINT(#HMI_PT_TP_s * 1000.0));

// TON — Timer On-Delay: Q_TON goes TRUE once Start (physical OR HMI
// memory tag) has been held TRUE for Preset_TON. Reset forces IN false,
// which instantly zeroes ET/Q.
#Timer_TON(IN := ("I_Start_TON" OR "HMI_Start_TON") AND NOT ("I_Reset_TON" OR "HMI_Reset_TON"),
           PT := #Preset_TON);
"Q_TON" := #Timer_TON.Q;
#HMI_ET_TON_s := TIME_TO_DINT(#Timer_TON.ET) / 1.0E+003;

// TOF — Timer Off-Delay: Q_TOF drops FALSE only Preset_TOF after Start
// goes FALSE (goes TRUE immediately when Start is pressed/energized).
// Reset forces IN false, same as releasing Start (delayed drop by PT).
#Timer_TOF(IN := ("I_Start_TOF" OR "HMI_Start_TOF") AND NOT ("I_Reset_TOF" OR "HMI_Reset_TOF"),
           PT := #Preset_TOF);
"Q_TOF" := #Timer_TOF.Q;
#HMI_ET_TOF_s := TIME_TO_DINT(#Timer_TOF.ET) / 1.0E+003;

// TP — Pulse timer: on a rising edge of Start, Q_TP stays TRUE for
// exactly Preset_TP, regardless of how long Start stays TRUE. Reset only
// blocks a NEW pulse from starting while held; cannot cut a running one
// short (TP ignores IN once triggered).
#Timer_TP(IN := ("I_Start_TP" OR "HMI_Start_TP") AND NOT ("I_Reset_TP" OR "HMI_Reset_TP"),
          PT := #Preset_TP);
"Q_TP" := #Timer_TP.Q;
#HMI_ET_TP_s := TIME_TO_DINT(#Timer_TP.ET) / 1.0E+003;

// CTU — Count Up: increments on each rising edge of CU (physical OR HMI
// memory tag); Q_CTU goes TRUE once CV >= Preset_CTU. R resets CV to 0
// (CTU natively supports R).
// NOTE 3: TIA's counter types (CTU_INT/CTD_INT/CTUD_INT) share one
// underlying structure with QU/QD outputs, not a plain "Q" — confirmed
// via autocomplete (CD, CU, CV, LD, PV, QD, QU, R; no "Q"). Use .QU for
// CTU's up-output, .QD for CTD's down-output.
#Counter_CTU(CU := "I_CU_CTU" OR "HMI_CU_CTU",
             R  := "I_Reset_CTU" OR "HMI_Reset_CTU",
             PV := #Preset_CTU);
"Q_CTU" := #Counter_CTU.QU;
#HMI_CV_CTU := #Counter_CTU.CV;

// CTD — Count Down: decrements on each rising edge of CD, starting from
// Preset_CTD; Q_CTD goes TRUE once CV <= 0. LD reloads CV with
// Preset_CTD (CTD has no R; LD is its reset/reload mechanism).
#Counter_CTD(CD := "I_CD_CTD" OR "HMI_CD_CTD",
             LD := "I_Load_CTD" OR "HMI_Load_CTD",
             PV := #Preset_CTD);
"Q_CTD" := #Counter_CTD.QD;
#HMI_CV_CTD := #Counter_CTD.CV;

// CTUD — Count Up/Down: CU increments, CD decrements; QU TRUE once
// CV >= Preset_CTUD, QD TRUE once CV <= 0. R forces CV to 0, LD
// loads CV with Preset_CTUD (CTUD natively supports both R and LD).
#Counter_CTUD(CU := "I_CU_CTUD" OR "HMI_CU_CTUD",
              CD := "I_CD_CTUD" OR "HMI_CD_CTUD",
              R  := "I_Reset_CTUD" OR "HMI_Reset_CTUD",
              LD := "I_Load_CTUD" OR "HMI_Load_CTUD",
              PV := #Preset_CTUD);
"Q_CTUD_QU" := #Counter_CTUD.QU;
"Q_CTUD_QD" := #Counter_CTUD.QD;
#HMI_CV_CTUD := #Counter_CTUD.CV;
